```

Wait, there's a subtle issue here. When `inf` is true (indefinite length inner element), `asn1_check_tlen` sets `plen = len - (p - q)` (the remaining data). Then `expected_eoc++`. But `p` is NOT advanced. The next iteration continues from the same `p`.

But what if the inner element has a definite length? Then `p += plen` advances past the content. And `len -= p - q` updates the remaining length.

The issue: when `inf` is true for the inner element, `p` is not advanced, so the next iteration will try to parse the same tag again! This would cause an infinite loop... unless `asn1_check_tlen` advances `p` past the tag and length.

Actually, looking at `asn1_check_tlen`:
```c
*p = p + hdrlen;
```
where `hdrlen` is the length of the tag and length bytes. So `p` IS advanced past the header, just not past the content (since the content length is indefinite).

So for indefinite length inner elements, `p` points to the start of the content after the header. The next iteration will parse the next element inside the indefinite content. This is correct.

